---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Quiz from '../../components/Quiz.astro';

const questions = [
  {
    question: "A script processes '10:00 to 10:10' using processing time. What goes wrong?",
    options: [
      "The script crashes immediately because processing time cannot be used in a WHERE clause",
      "Every event delayed by the network lands in the wrong bucket, no matter how short the interval is",
      "The problem only appears if the script runs less often than once per minute",
      "Nothing goes wrong, since processing time and event time always match within a 10-minute window"
    ],
    correct: 1,
    explanation: "Bucketing by processing time misplaces any event that was delayed, regardless of how short the batch interval is. The interval length does not fix a clock problem."
  },
  {
    question: "If the same script switches to event time instead, what new problem appears?",
    options: [
      "It may need to reopen and recompute previous runs whenever a late event arrives, so it stops being a simple 'last 10 minutes' job",
      "It can no longer connect to Kafka, since event time requires a different broker",
      "It becomes strictly faster than any dedicated streaming engine",
      "It loses the ability to compute any aggregate at all"
    ],
    correct: 0,
    explanation: "Using event time correctly means the script has to be able to revisit and recompute earlier windows when late data shows up, which is exactly the complexity a real streaming engine is built to handle."
  },
  {
    question: "Why does a micro-batch script computing 'unique users today' get more expensive as the day goes on?",
    options: [
      "Because Postgres automatically locks the users table after every run",
      "Because the schema for the users table changes every 10 minutes",
      "Because it re-reads everything from midnight to now on every run, instead of keeping incremental state",
      "Because Kafka topics used for this metric shrink in size over the course of a day"
    ],
    correct: 2,
    explanation: "Without incremental state, a cumulative metric forces the script to rescan the whole day so far, every single run, so the cost grows as the day progresses."
  },
  {
    question: "How does a real streaming engine avoid the growing rescan cost described above?",
    options: [
      "It keeps live intermediate state and updates it incrementally, one event at a time",
      "It deletes all previously counted users at the end of each day",
      "It runs the exact same rescan logic, just on more powerful hardware",
      "It skips counting any user who was already seen the previous day"
    ],
    correct: 0,
    explanation: "A streaming engine updates its running total incrementally as each event arrives, instead of recomputing the whole history on a schedule."
  },
  {
    question: "For a fraud alert or a temperature alarm, what is the risk of waiting for the next scheduled batch run?",
    options: [
      "There is no real risk, since alerts of this kind are rarely time sensitive",
      "The reaction can arrive too late to matter",
      "The alert gets duplicated once for every scheduled run since the last trigger",
      "The alert silently gets routed to the wrong monitoring dashboard"
    ],
    correct: 1,
    explanation: "Time-sensitive reactions need to happen close to the moment the condition occurs. Waiting for the next scheduled run can make the alert useless by the time it fires."
  },
  {
    question: "According to this pill, do these three problems (boundaries, growing state cost, latency and idempotency) get smaller if you shorten the batch interval to 1 minute?",
    options: [
      "Yes, a short enough interval effectively eliminates all three problems",
      "Only the boundary problem improves, while the other two stay the same",
      "Only the state cost problem improves, while the other two get worse",
      "No, they are architectural problems, not a matter of running more often"
    ],
    correct: 3,
    explanation: "None of these three problems are about speed. They come from the architecture itself: which clock is trusted, whether state is kept incrementally, and whether correctness guarantees are built in."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Quiz: Micro-Batching | Streaming Pill 4"
  description="Test your understanding of why a scheduled script is not the same as streaming, and the three problems that prove it."
>
  <section class="pill-quiz-page">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/blog/streaming-pill-4-micro-batching-is-not-streaming" class="back-link">← Back to Pill 4</a>

      <div class="pill-header">
        <div class="badge badge-series" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Pill 4 · Micro-Batching</div>
        <h1>Quiz: Is Every 10 Minutes Streaming?</h1>
        <p class="pill-lead">
          These questions test whether you can name the three concrete problems a scheduled
          script runs into, not just repeat that "micro-batching is bad."
        </p>
      </div>

      <Quiz
        title="Pill 4: Micro-Batching Is Not Streaming"
        questions={questions}
      />

      <div class="pill-footer glass-card">
        <h3>How did it go?</h3>
        <p>
          If you scored below 70%, re-read <strong>Pill 4</strong>, and think of a scheduled
          script you have seen in production. Which of the three problems would it hit first?
        </p>
        <div class="pill-footer-links">
          <a href="/blog/streaming-pill-4-micro-batching-is-not-streaming" class="btn btn-secondary btn-sm">Re-read Pill 4</a>
          <a href="/pills/streaming-quiz-5" class="btn btn-primary btn-sm">Next: Pill 5 Quiz — State Bottleneck</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .pill-quiz-page {
    padding: 3.5rem 0 5rem;
  }

  .back-link {
    display: inline-block;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.8rem;
    transition: color 0.2s ease;
  }
  .back-link:hover {
    color: var(--accent-blue);
  }

  .badge-series {
    background: var(--accent-blue);
    color: #ffffff;
  }

  .pill-header {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .pill-header h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3.5vw, 2.2rem);
    margin-bottom: 0.8rem;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .pill-lead {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
    max-width: 640px;
  }

  .pill-footer {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    padding: 2rem;
    background: var(--bg-card);
  }

  .pill-footer h3 {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    color: var(--text-main);
  }

  .pill-footer p {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: 1.1rem;
    max-width: 600px;
  }

  .pill-footer-links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.65rem;
  }
</style>
